      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(\\cos 135^\\circ\\), we can use the unit circle and the properties of trigonometric functions. The angle \\(135^\\circ\\) is in the second quadrant, where the cosine function is negative. We can express \\(135^\\circ\\) as \\(180^\\circ - 45^\\circ\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 135^\\circ = \\cos (180^\\circ - 45^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nUsing the cosine identity for the difference of angles, \\(\\cos (180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\cos \\theta\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 135^\\circ = \\cos (180^\\circ - 45^\\circ) = -\\cos 45^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\cos 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 135^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\n\\]",
